### Tawseela API — auth smoke tests
### Prereqs: Postgres localhost:55432, DB `mydb`, user `admin` — run sql/schema.sql first, app :8080
###
### OTP behaviour:
###   sms.enabled=false (local)  → OTP printed in the Spring log: look for "OTP for +xx... → 123456"
###   sms.enabled=true  → OTP also sent via Twilio SMS (needs TWILIO_* env vars + your Twilio phone number)

@base = http://localhost:8080
@phone = +213555000099

### 1) Request OTP — OTP appears in the console log when SMS is disabled
POST {{base}}/api/v1/auth/otp/request
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "phone": "{{phone}}"
}

### 2) Verify OTP → register (new phone) OR login (existing) → returns JWTs
###    Copy the 6-digit code from the log (or SMS) and paste it in "otp" below.
###    "role" is only applied for NEW users: "customer" (default) or "driver". Cannot set "admin" here.
###    "fullName", "email", "fcmToken" are all optional and stored/updated on every verify.
POST {{base}}/api/v1/auth/otp/verify
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "phone": "{{phone}}",
  "otp": "PASTE_6_DIGIT_CODE_FROM_LOG",
  "role": "customer",
  "fullName": "Test User",
  "email": "test.user@example.com",
  "fcmToken": null
}

### Token notes:
###   accessToken  → valid 1 hour  (use in Authorization header)
###   refreshToken → valid 7 days  (use to get a fresh pair via /token/refresh)
###   user.role    → customer | driver | admin
###   user.email   → real email if provided, otherwise {digits}@phone.tawseela.local

### @access = paste accessToken from step 2

### 3) Current user
GET {{base}}/api/v1/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer REPLACE_WITH_ACCESS_TOKEN

### 4) Refresh tokens
POST {{base}}/api/v1/auth/token/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "REPLACE_WITH_REFRESH_TOKEN"
}

### 5) Logout (stateless — 204, client must discard both tokens)
POST {{base}}/api/v1/auth/logout
Authorization: Bearer REPLACE_WITH_ACCESS_TOKEN
